Subject: [PATCH] easystick: esp32 ACM: take the port lock for TX in the IRQ handler (#5)

The USB Serial/JTAG IRQ handler fills the TX FIFO from the xmit kfifo
without the port lock, while start_tx() does the same under it.  On one
CPU the two never overlap, because the lock is taken with interrupts
off.  With maxcpus=2 the handler on one CPU and start_tx() on the other
can both run kfifo_get(): each sends the same byte and each increments
out, so a byte goes out twice, the next one is skipped, and out can
pass in.  kfifo_len() is then about 4G, and the port keeps sending its
whole 4 KiB buffer (old console output and NULs) at full USB speed.
Four of the first eight maxcpus=2 boots with 0079 did this, at about
850 KB/s until the capture ended.  In three of them the last line before
the replay already shows the doubled and missing bytes, such as
"GOTGCTL==xx00990000" (the real value is 0x00090000).

rxint() already takes the lock; take it in txint() too.  esp32_uart.c
already takes it around txint() in its IRQ handler (0001).

---
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/esp32_acm.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/esp32_acm.c
@@ -177,7 +177,16 @@
 
 static void esp32s3_acm_txint(struct uart_port *port)
 {
+	unsigned long flags;
+
+	/*
+	 * start_tx() runs under the port lock; without it here, the two
+	 * can drain the xmit kfifo at once on SMP and push its out index
+	 * past in, so the whole buffer is sent again and again.
+	 */
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&port->lock, flags);
 	esp32s3_acm_transmit_buffer(port);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&port->lock, flags);
 }
 
 static irqreturn_t esp32s3_acm_int(int irq, void *dev_id)
